<h2>Plan changes</h2>

<h3>Upgrades</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Adding a feature mid-cycle (blog, calculator, integration): quote separately. Bill on completion or roll into next month.</li>
  <li>Adding new pages over the 10-page allotment: $150 each, billed on the next monthly invoice.</li>
  <li>Adding a new service-area page: free if it slots into the existing data file pattern; quoted if custom.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Downgrades</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Switching from Monthly to Lump-Sum: rare. Per the agreement, requires explicit written request. Quote a buyout: typically (months_remaining x $175) plus a one-time code-handover fee.</li>
  <li>Reducing scope (e.g. dropping a feature we built): we don't refund retroactively for work already done.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Pause / hibernation</h3>
<ul>
  <li>If client's business pauses (seasonal, life event), offer a 50% maintenance rate that keeps the site live with no content updates. Document the pause window in the agreement amendment.</li>
  <li>Re-activation requires written notice + a return-to-service fee if pause exceeded 6 months.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Refunds</h2>

<p>Refunds happen rarely. When they do:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Within 30 days of launch + client genuinely unhappy:</strong> consider a partial refund (e.g. last month's monthly fee). Document the reason. Don't argue.</li>
  <li><strong>Outside 30 days:</strong> per the agreement, no refunds for completed work. Offer a credit toward future months instead if circumstances warrant.</li>
  <li><strong>Service issue (we missed a deliverable):</strong> we own it. Refund the affected month, not the entire engagement.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Write-offs</h2>
<p>If a client genuinely can't pay and the relationship is over: write off the balance after day 60. Mark in CRM as "uncollectible". Move on. Don't send to collections; the relationship and the reputation are worth more than $175.</p>

<h2>Reading your monthly invoice (client-facing)</h2>
<p>Reference the public <a href="/owners-guide/#invoice">/owners-guide/#invoice</a> when clients ask what's on their invoice. Single line item, one price, no surprise add-ons. If something looks weird, we explain in one email; we've never had a billing dispute go past one email.</p>

<h2>Accounting basics</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Quickbooks for bookkeeping. Enter every charge + expense weekly.</li>
  <li>Save 1099s for any contractor &gt; $600/year.</li>
  <li>Quarterly estimated tax payments (federal + state).</li>
  <li>End-of-year P&amp;L statement to the accountant by Jan 15.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common billing issues</h2>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>Card-on-file fails repeatedly</strong>
  Three declines in a row, escalate to phone call. Most cause: card got reissued. Quick fix.
</div>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>Client disputes the invoice</strong>
  Reply same day with the line-item explanation. Reference the agreement. Almost always resolved by being specific about what they're paying for. If they still dispute, refund the disputed amount and reset; the relationship is worth more.
</div>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>Client wants to renegotiate mid-term</strong>
  Listen. If the business has changed shape (downsized, pivoted), the right answer might be a downgrade or a pause, not a hard "no". The agreement covers term length but our reputation covers everything.
</div>

<div class="sop-callout">
  <strong>Client is consistently late but always pays</strong>
  Drop them a friendly email. Offer to switch them to card-on-file to remove the friction. Not a big deal.
</div>

<h2>Exit criteria</h2>
<p>Billing is steady-state, like maintenance. Doesn't have a clean exit; only the client leaving (phase 12) ends the cycle.</p>
